<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hou Junji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 643</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hou Junji was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> general who served the emperor Taizong. He led campaigns against the Tuyuhun and in 640 conquered the oasis kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, which became the Tang prefecture of Xizhou. Implicated in a plot by the crown prince Li Chengqian, he was executed in 643.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hou-junji.jpg" alt="Hou Junji" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 閻立本, 劉源 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BE%AF%E5%90%9B%E9%9B%86%EF%BC%88%E6%B7%A9%E7%85%99%E9%96%A3%E5%8A%9F%E8%87%A3%E5%9C%96%E5%83%8F%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hou Junji's date of birth is unknown, and little is known of his family except that he came from Bin Prefecture, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. He was said to be pretentious and eager to impress, and became known as a fighter rather than as an archer. Early in the reign of Emperor Gaozu he entered the service of Gaozu's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, the Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, who made him Viscount of Quanjiao and often heard his advice. In 626, with Zhangsun Wuji, Gao Shilian and Yuchi Gong, he urged Li Shimin to strike first against his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanji">Li Yuanji</a>, and he led Li Shimin's forces in the fighting that followed their killing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> took the throne as Emperor Taizong, he placed Hou among the five officials of the highest merit and made him Duke of Lu. In 630 Hou became minister of defence and a de facto chancellor. In 634 and 635 he served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> against the Tuyuhun; when the enemy burned the grazing, Hou argued for advancing, and the campaign ended in victory. In 637 his title became Duke of Chen. In 638 he commanded the counter-attack against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> of Tufan.</p><p>Around the new year of 640 Taizong sent Hou against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-wentai">Qu Wentai</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, who had allied with the Western Tujue. Qu Wentai died as the army arrived, and Hou besieged the capital until his son Qu Zhisheng surrendered. On his return to Chang'an Hou was accused of taking treasure from Gaochang's treasury and enslaving captives, and was briefly arrested before Cen Wenben persuaded the emperor to release him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Resentful at this treatment, Hou joined the plot of the crown prince Li Chengqian through his son-in-law Helan Chushi, the commander of the prince's guards. When the guard Gegan Chengji betrayed the plot, the conspirators were sentenced to death. Taizong wished to spare Hou, but his officials objected, and the emperor and Hou wept as they parted. Hou was beheaded on 29 April 643; his wife and children were pardoned but exiled to the region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. In the same year his portrait had been among the Portraits at Lingyan Pavilion that Taizong commissioned to honour 24 great contributors to Tang rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hou Junji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 640 the Tang Dynasty sent <b>Hou Junji</b>, Qu Wentai died as the army approached, and his son surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xunyi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.11,108.33&amp;year=633" aria-label="Xunyi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hou Junji"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4189 4522h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4189 4522h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.11,108.33&amp;year=633">What was here in 633 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
